Why Prototypes are Needed: C compilers read code from top to bottom. If main() calls a function defined below it without a prior prototype, the compiler issues a warning or compilation error because it does not know the return type or parameter signature.


GOTO 
goto statement is for going to that 
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Line 1: Start\n");

    goto skip; // Jumps directly to 'skip'

    printf("Line 2: This will be skipped!\n");
    printf("Line 3: This will also be skipped!\n");

skip: // Destination label (ends with a colon)
    printf("Line 4: Landed at skip label\n");

    return 0;
}

#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 1;

repeat: // Label
    printf("%d ", i);
    i++;

    if (i <= 5) {
        goto repeat; // Backward jump
    }

    return 0;
}
Why Discouraged ("Spaghetti Code"): Using multiple goto statements causes tangled, jumping control paths that resemble a bowl of spaghetti. It violates structured programming by bypassing single-entry/single-exit structures, making tracing and debugging extremely difficult.